export function pageMetadata({ title, description, path }: { title: string; description: string; path: string }): Metadata {
  return {
    title,
    description,
    alternates: { canonical: path },
    openGraph: {
      title,
      description,
      url: path,
      siteName: SITE_NAME,
      type: "website",
    },
    twitter: {
      card: "summary_large_image",
      title,
      description,
    },
  };
}
```

Wait—if each page returns `openGraph` without images, will the file-based opengraph-image be included? Risk. Let me think about whether to include images.

Actually, let me reconsider: instead of a helper returning openGraph, I could NOT set openGraph in child pages at all, letting root openGraph inherit (with root title/desc) plus the file-based image. But then og:title/desc are generic.

Hmm. Let me look at how Next.js resolves this more concretely. The doc says: file-based metadata has higher priority and overrides the metadata object. That implies the file-based opengraph-image is the authoritative source for og:image and will be added regardless. Also, metadata resolution merges the `metadata` objects across segments shallowly, and then file-based metadata is applied. So if a child sets openGraph, it replaces the parent's openGraph object for og:title/desc, but the file-based image from the ancestor still applies because it's inherited (the child has no opengraph-image file). 

I'm fairly confident. But to be extra safe, I can ALSO include the image in each page's openGraph via the explicit generated route. But the route is unstable.

Alternatively, I could avoid the file-convention entirely and instead create a real static OG image. I can generate a PNG using ImageResponse at build... no.

You know what, the cleanest guaranteed approach: use the file convention at root (app/opengraph-image.tsx) and DO NOT set `openGraph.images` anywhere. Trust that the file-based image is inherited. This is the documented, idiomatic approach. Many Next.js apps do exactly this. I'll go with it.

Actually, let me reconsider even simpler: I'll set per-page openGraph with title/description/url. That's standard. The inherited og:image remains. Done.

Hmm, but there's a subtlety I should verify: does `openGraph.type` need to be set? If child sets openGraph without `type`, it may inherit? Shallow replace means type would be lost (undefined). Not critical. I'll include type: "website" in the helper.

Let me also add `twitter` per page. Since twitter is also shallow-merged, include card.

Now, the helper approach: all child pages use `pageMetadata({title, description, path})`. This is clean and DRY.

